      "type": "edge_case",
      "co_persona": "EC2b",
      "quote": "I want to stay part of this community. I just need to know that if I can't manage it myself one day, Margaret can do it for me — and that it will still be my identity, not hers.",
      "context": {
        "age": 74,
        "location": "Sheffield, UK",
        "diagnosis": "Early-stage vascular dementia; capacity intermittent and progressive",
        "capacity_status": "Currently has capacity; LPA registered but not yet primary decision-making instrument"
      },
      "lifecycle_phases": [
        "Ongoing",
        "Revocation",
        "Expiration"
      ],
      "goals": [
        "Remain an active, recognised VTC member for as long as he has capacity",
        "Ensure his attorney can take over credential management seamlessly on capacity loss",
        "Retain the sense that his trust graph identity is his own, not a proxy created for his carer",
        "Not have his diagnosis disclosed or embedded in credential metadata without explicit consent"
      ],
      "risk_context": [
        "Credential architecture has no concept of progressive, variable capacity to consent",
        "LPA translation gap — a registered Lasting Power of Attorney has no DTG credential equivalent",
        "Continuity of identity through a capacity transition is unspecified",
        "GR-08 revocation due process assumes the member can receive and respond to notice",
        "Intermittent capacity is a higher-value social engineering target for B1 and M2"
      ],
      "inclusion_drivers": [
        "LPA-equivalent delegation credential",
        "Capacity-sensitive revocation due process routed to the attorney",
        "Identity continuity on delegation — M-DID persists through transition"
      ],
      "exclusion_risks": [
        "Progressive capacity loss makes trust task ceremonies unusable",
        "A VTC that cannot model guardianship defaults to treating him as full-capacity until a crisis"
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "Around 982,000 people are living with dementia in the UK, with over a third lacking a formal diagnosis. The Mental Capacity Act 2005 requires capacity to be assessed decision-by-decision.",
          "source": "Alzheimer's Society, Dementia Action Week 2025",
          "url": "https://www.alzheimers.org.uk/"
        },
        {
          "claim": "An LPA can only be created while the person still has capacity; once lost, the costlier Court of Protection deputyship route is required.",
          "source": "Dementia UK, Lasting Power of Attorney, 2025",
          "url": "https://www.dementiauk.org/information-and-support/financial-and-legal-support/lasting-power-of-attorney/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/EC2b",
      "@type": "Persona",
      "name": "Margaret Kowalczyk",
      "role": "Partner, Carer and registered LPA holder",
      "type": "edge_case",
      "co_persona": "EC2a",
      "quote": "The law says I can make decisions for Arthur when he can't. But the system doesn't know I exist. It just sees his DID going quiet.",
      "context": {
        "age": 72,
        "location": "Sheffield, UK",
        "dtg_role": "Delegated credential operator for EC2a's M-DID; not an independent VTC member",
        "legal_basis": "Registered Health and Welfare LPA and Property and Financial Affairs LPA"
      },
      "lifecycle_phases": [
        "Ongoing",
        "Revocation"
      ],
      "goals": [
        "Exercise LPA authority over credentials in a legally sound, auditable and reversible way",
        "Preserve the donor's VTC membership and trust graph relationships through capacity transitions",
        "Receive governance communications, including revocation notices, on the donor's behalf",
        "Avoid personal liability for operations performed in good faith as attorney"
      ],
      "risk_context": [
        "No legal recognition of the LPA in the DTG architecture",
        "Authentication challenge — the VTA cannot distinguish a legitimate LPA holder from account takeover",
        "Notification gap — no support for secondary notification recipients",
        "Liability without framework — no audit trail to demonstrate Mental Capacity Act compliance",
        "Credential scope creep — an over-broad delegation could forge the donor's trust relationships"
      ],
      "inclusion_drivers": [
        "LPA-equivalent delegation credential registered in the trust registry, scope-limited and separately audited",
        "Secondary notification registration for credential lifecycle communications",
        "Clear revocation pathway restoring sole control if capacity returns"
      ],
      "exclusion_risks": [
        "Without a formal delegation model she is invisible to the system and cannot be involved in due process",
        "No defined pathway to close the donor's M-DID on death or preserve membership history"
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "The Mental Capacity Act 2005 requires attorneys to act in the donor's best interests and keep clear records; the NHS requires hospitals to be informed of LPA status as early as possible.",
          "source": "NHS, Managing Legal Affairs After a Dementia Diagnosis",
          "url": "https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/dementia/care-and-support/legal-issues/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/EC3",
      "@type": "Persona",
      "name": "Northgate Community Health Trust",
      "role": "Public sector healthcare provider — organisational member",
      "type": "edge_case",
      "organisational": true,
      "quote": "We are not a person. We are a regulated organisation with a board, a Caldicott Guardian, and a statutory duty of care. Our credential needs to carry the weight of that accountability.",
      "context": {
        "entity_type": "NHS Foundation Trust (fictitious); CQC registered",
        "jurisdiction": "England — NHS England regulatory framework",
        "dtg_role": "Organisational VTC member holding a C-DID; issuing authority for staff M-DIDs and VMCs",
        "accountable_officers": [
          "Caldicott Guardian",
          "Data Protection Officer",
          "Chief Digital Officer"
        ],
        "regulatory_context": "NHS DCB3051 Identity Verification and Authentication Standard (updated December 2024 to align with GPG45); UK GDPR; NHS Care Identity Service 2 (CIS2)"
      },
      "lifecycle_phases": [
        "Phase 2",
        "Phase 3",
        "Phase 4",
        "Revocation"
      ],
      "goals": [
        "Issue verifiable staff credentials interoperable across NHS Trusts and recognised by CIS2",
        "Participate as a trusted institutional member so staff avoid paper-based verification",
        "Maintain compliance with the NHS identity standard and the Data Security and Protection Toolkit",
        "Revoke a leaver's VMC promptly with revocation visible to NHS relying parties"
      ],
      "risk_context": [
        "Organisational identity is not a natural person — the C-DID model assumes capacity to consent",
        "Trust anchor accountability sits personally with the Caldicott Guardian and CDO",
        "Revocation at scale — the NHS Digital Staff Passport requires near-real-time revocation",
        "Multi-jurisdictional staff working across Trust boundaries with differing implementations",
        "Data residency — NHS patient-adjacent data cannot leave UK jurisdiction"
      ],
      "inclusion_drivers": [
        "Interoperability with NHS CIS2",
        "Governance conformance class satisfied by existing statutory governance, properly documented",
        "Caldicott Guardian as trust anchor — a coherent mapping of an existing NHS data governance role"
      ],
      "exclusion_risks": [
        "The DTG specification does not define an organisational credential type",
        "NHS procurement and information governance cycles (12–18 months) are incompatible with DTG cadence"
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "The NHS Digital Staff Passport enabled rapid staff redeployment across 100 NHS Trusts using verifiable credentials, moving to public beta in 2025. Lessons — wallet-agnosticism is essential; issuance must connect to existing HR systems; multi-organisational trust governance is complex.",
          "source": "techUK / NHSx-Condatis case study",
          "url": "https://www.techuk.org/resource/healthcare-decentralised-identity-case-study-nhsx.html"
        },
        {
          "claim": "NHS organisations are subject to DCB3051 and CIS2; the DTG trust registry concept maps onto the NHS Credentials Gateway but the integration is unspecified.",
          "source": "NHS Standards Directory, Identity Verification and Authentication Standard",
          "url": "https://standards.nhs.uk/published-standards/identity-verification-and-authentication-standard-for-digital-health-and-care-services"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://trustoverip.github.io/dtgwg-rahp-tf/id/EC4",
      "@type": "Persona",
      "name": "Velvet Gate Ltd",
      "role": "Age-restricted content service provider — relying party",
      "type": "edge_case",
      "organisational": true,
      "safeguarding": true,
      "safeguarding_note": "Included because this sector drives the UK's Online Safety Act 2023 age verification requirements, making it the most demanding real-world test of the DTG identity verification architecture. Presented analytically. All design decisions for this persona must prioritise child safety and comply with the Online Safety Act 2023 and Ofcom age assurance guidance effective 25 July 2025.",
      "quote": "We have a legal obligation to verify that every subscriber is over 18. We have a competing legal obligation not to build a surveillance database of who watches what.",
      "context": {
        "entity_type": "UK-registered SME; Part 5 service under the Online Safety Act 2023 (fictitious)",
        "jurisdiction": "UK-registered, serving UK and international subscribers; regulated by Ofcom",
        "dtg_role": "Relying party — does not issue VMCs, relies on age-verified IDVCs from approved IDVPs",
        "regulatory_context": "Online Safety Act 2023 Part 5; mandatory age assurance from 25 July 2025; UK GDPR; Ofcom Children's Code"
      },
      "lifecycle_phases": [
        "Phase 4",
        "Ongoing"
      ],
      "goals": [
        "Satisfy Ofcom's highly effective age assurance requirement without a persistent identity database",
        "Use ZKP-based proof of being over 18 to comply with the Online Safety Act and UK GDPR simultaneously",
        "Achieve compliance that is technically auditable by Ofcom",
        "Reduce exposure to fines of up to £18m or 10% of global qualifying revenue"
      ],
      "risk_context": [
        "Surveillance vs compliance paradox — robust age verification versus data minimisation",
        "IDVP quality risk — IDVP failure cascades onto the relying party (see AU10TIX, 2024)",
        "Bad actor abuse — automated fake-age-credential generation at scale by B1 and M2",
        "Child safety is non-negotiable — an architecture gap allowing a minor to obtain a valid over-18 IDVC is a criminal liability, not merely a governance risk",
        "Cross-border enforcement — non-UK IDVP standing in the trust registry is uncertain"
      ],
      "inclusion_drivers": [
        "ZKP-based age proofs satisfying both Ofcom age assurance and UK GDPR data minimisation",
        "Well-governed IDVP approval process giving a defensible basis for third-party reliance",
        "Ofcom's accepted methods include digital ID services, encompassing W3C VC-based approaches"
      ],
      "exclusion_risks": [
        "If ZKP implementation remains future work (as in spec v0.3), it cannot be relied on for compliance",
        "Reputational risk may make some IDVPs and anchors unwilling to participate"
      ],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "claim": "From 25 July 2025 Ofcom enforces highly effective age assurance with fines up to £18m or 10% of global qualifying revenue; accepted methods include digital ID services but must be technically accurate, robust, reliable and fair.",
          "source": "Ofcom, Age Checks for Online Safety, June 2025",
          "url": "https://www.ofcom.org.uk/online-safety/protecting-children/age-checks-for-online-safety--what-you-need-to-know-as-a-user"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The AU10TIX breach illustrates what happens when age verification relies on centralised IDVP databases rather than holder-controlled zero-knowledge proofs.",
          "source": "Lawfare, To Read This Please Upload Photo ID, 2026",
          "url": "https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/to-read-this--please-upload-photo-id"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
